1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:02,307
Hi everyone.

2
00:00:02,307 --> 00:00:08,161
So today we are going to continue where Andrej Karpathy's Zero to Hero series left off.

3
00:00:08,161 --> 00:00:13,879
If you've been following along, you've built micrograd, you've built makemore, you've

4
00:00:13,879 --> 00:00:19,867
built a GPT from scratch on tiny Shakespeare, and in the last video you reproduced GPT-2,

5
00:00:19,867 --> 00:00:24,576
the 124 million parameter version, and you watched the loss come down.

6
00:00:24,576 --> 00:00:27,873
And at the end of all that, you had a base model.

7
00:00:27,873 --> 00:00:29,959
A thing that can continue text.

8
00:00:29,959 --> 00:00:35,745
And it's really cool, but if you actually try to talk to it, you'll find that it's not

9
00:00:35,745 --> 00:00:36,283
ChatGPT.

10
00:00:36,283 --> 00:00:37,763
It's not an assistant.

11
00:00:37,763 --> 00:00:39,378
It just sort of babbles.

12
00:00:39,378 --> 00:00:43,257
So the question for today is: what happens after pretraining?

13
00:00:43,257 --> 00:00:48,980
How do you go from a base model, an internet document simulator, to something that you can

14
00:00:48,980 --> 00:00:54,640
actually talk to, that answers your questions, that uses tools, that solves math problems

15
00:00:54,640 --> 00:00:58,202
and that can even count the number of r's in strawberry?

16
00:00:58,202 --> 00:01:03,480
This stage is called post-training, and today we are going to build all of it, from

17
00:01:03,480 --> 00:01:07,041
scratch, in the style of Karpathy's nanochat repository.

18
00:01:07,041 --> 00:01:13,054
Now nanochat is the full-stack ChatGPT clone that does everything, tokenizer training,

19
00:01:13,054 --> 00:01:19,347
pretraining, supervised finetuning, reinforcement learning, evaluation and inference, in a

20
00:01:19,347 --> 00:01:21,025
single minimal codebase.

21
00:01:21,025 --> 00:01:23,402
Pretraining is the expensive part.

22
00:01:23,402 --> 00:01:27,387
It needs a node of eight H100 GPUs for a couple of hours.

23
00:01:27,387 --> 00:01:33,470
But post-training is much cheaper, and so what we're going to do is we're going to take

24
00:01:33,470 --> 00:01:39,483
the GPT-2 base model that you already know and love, and we're going to put it through

25
00:01:39,483 --> 00:01:42,769
exactly the post-training pipeline of nanochat.

26
00:01:42,769 --> 00:01:48,922
Every file we write today has a counterpart in nanochat, with the same name and the same

27
00:01:48,922 --> 00:01:54,166
structure, and at the end I'll show you how to run the real thing at scale.

28
00:01:54,166 --> 00:01:55,663
So here is the roadmap.

29
00:01:55,663 --> 00:02:01,065
First, we'll look at what a base model actually does, and we'll see why it's not an

30
00:02:01,065 --> 00:02:01,715
assistant.

31
00:02:01,715 --> 00:02:07,442
Then we'll figure out how to represent a conversation as a sequence of tokens, with some

32
00:02:07,442 --> 00:02:08,679
new special tokens.

33
00:02:08,679 --> 00:02:14,015
Then we'll assemble a mixture of training data, write a dataloader for it, and run

34
00:02:14,015 --> 00:02:15,968
supervised finetuning, or SFT.

35
00:02:15,968 --> 00:02:21,044
Then we'll write an inference engine with a KV cache and a little python tool.

36
00:02:21,044 --> 00:02:25,274
Then we'll write some evaluations so we can see what we're doing.

37
00:02:25,274 --> 00:02:30,936
And finally, we'll do reinforcement learning, where the model practices on problems and

38
00:02:30,936 --> 00:02:33,735
learns from its own successes and failures.

39
00:02:33,735 --> 00:02:35,492
Okay, so let's get started.

40
00:02:36,992 --> 00:02:39,624
Okay so first, let's load up GPT-2.

41
00:02:39,624 --> 00:02:46,243
I've written a gpt.py file here, and this is basically exactly the GPT-2 module from the

42
00:02:46,243 --> 00:02:48,123
GPT-2 reproduction video.

43
00:02:48,123 --> 00:02:54,817
There's the token embeddings, the position embeddings, the twelve transformer blocks, the

44
00:02:54,817 --> 00:03:01,210
final layer norm and the language model head, which shares its weights with the token

45
00:03:01,210 --> 00:03:01,963
embedding.

46
00:03:01,963 --> 00:03:08,732
I've only added two things to it, a KV cache, and a few extra rows in the embedding table,

47
00:03:08,732 --> 00:03:12,191
and we'll come back to both of those in a bit.

48
00:03:12,191 --> 00:03:16,855
For now let's just load the OpenAI weights and sample from it.

49
00:03:17,855 --> 00:03:24,170
So here I'm doing greedy decoding, so always taking the most likely next token, and you'll

50
00:03:24,170 --> 00:03:25,504
recognize this one.

51
00:03:25,504 --> 00:03:31,468
"Hello, I'm a language model, and I'm a programmer." That's the same output as in the

52
00:03:31,468 --> 00:03:36,872
GPT-2 reproduction video, so we know that we've loaded the weights correctly.

53
00:03:36,872 --> 00:03:39,327
Now let's ask it a question.

54
00:03:40,327 --> 00:03:42,697
Okay, so actually this is not too bad.

55
00:03:42,697 --> 00:03:45,752
It does know that the capital of France is Paris.

56
00:03:45,752 --> 00:03:49,306
But then it says it again, and again, and again, forever.

57
00:03:49,306 --> 00:03:53,360
And that's because this is not an assistant answering a question.

58
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:55,168
This is a document simulator.

59
00:03:55,168 --> 00:04:00,655
It's seen a question on the internet, and it's asking itself, statistically, what tokens

60
00:04:00,655 --> 00:04:03,523
come next in a web page that starts like this.

61
00:04:03,523 --> 00:04:08,200
It has no notion of a question being answered, and no notion of being done.

62
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:09,692
So here's a fun trick.

63
00:04:09,692 --> 00:04:15,525
Because the base model is a document simulator, we can actually trick it into being an

64
00:04:15,525 --> 00:04:21,628
assistant, by giving it the beginning of a document that looks like a conversation with an

65
00:04:21,628 --> 00:04:22,307
assistant.

66
00:04:22,307 --> 00:04:25,291
This is sometimes called few-shot prompting.

67
00:04:25,291 --> 00:04:26,172
Let's try it.

68
00:04:27,172 --> 00:04:29,873
So look at this, this is really interesting.

69
00:04:29,873 --> 00:04:32,390
First of all, it picked up on the format.

70
00:04:32,390 --> 00:04:34,907
It answers in the voice of the Assistant.

71
00:04:34,907 --> 00:04:39,081
But the answer is "France", which is wrong, it's a very small model.

72
00:04:39,081 --> 00:04:40,923
And then, it just keeps going.

73
00:04:40,923 --> 00:04:46,325
It writes the next question from the Human, and then the answer of the Assistant, and so

74
00:04:46,325 --> 00:04:46,509
on.

75
00:04:46,509 --> 00:04:51,973
It's simulating the whole document, both sides of the conversation, because that's all it

76
00:04:51,973 --> 00:04:52,955
knows how to do.

77
00:04:52,955 --> 00:04:55,006
So this tells us a few things.

78
00:04:55,006 --> 00:05:01,159
Number one, the knowledge is in there, the capabilities are mostly there from pretraining.

79
00:05:01,159 --> 00:05:07,175
Number two, the base model is perfectly happy to adopt the persona of an assistant if it

80
00:05:07,175 --> 00:05:10,115
thinks that's the kind of document it's in.

81
00:05:10,115 --> 00:05:13,738
And number three, it has no idea where its turn ends.

82
00:05:13,738 --> 00:05:19,686
So the idea of post-training is basically: instead of tricking the model with a prompt,

83
00:05:19,686 --> 00:05:24,881
let's just continue training it on a lot of documents that look exactly like

84
00:05:24,881 --> 00:05:29,735
conversations, in some very specific format, with clearly marked turns.

85
00:05:29,735 --> 00:05:35,751
Then the model's statistics shift, and conversations become the only kind of document it

86
00:05:35,751 --> 00:05:36,162
knows.

87
00:05:36,162 --> 00:05:40,799
Exercise: Before we move on, think about how you would do this.

88
00:05:40,799 --> 00:05:47,424
If a conversation has to become a single sequence of tokens, what would that sequence look

89
00:05:47,424 --> 00:05:47,792
like?

90
00:05:47,792 --> 00:05:52,650
And which of those tokens should the model actually be trained on?

91
00:05:52,650 --> 00:05:55,153
Pause the video and give it a try.

92
00:05:58,653 --> 00:06:03,136
Okay so let's look at what a conversation looks like as data.

93
00:06:03,136 --> 00:06:09,456
In nanochat, and also in most of the datasets on HuggingFace, a conversation is just a

94
00:06:09,456 --> 00:06:15,776
dict with a list of messages, and each message has a role, user or assistant, and some

95
00:06:15,776 --> 00:06:16,364
content.

96
00:06:17,364 --> 00:06:21,672
And the question is how do we flatten this into one sequence of tokens.

97
00:06:21,672 --> 00:06:26,891
What we're going to do is we're going to introduce new special tokens that delimit the

98
00:06:26,891 --> 00:06:27,255
turns.

99
00:06:27,255 --> 00:06:32,474
There's a user start and a user end, an assistant start and an assistant end, and then

100
00:06:32,474 --> 00:06:37,935
there are two more pairs that we'll need later for tool use, python start and end for when

101
00:06:37,935 --> 00:06:43,276
the assistant wants to run some python code, and output start and end, for the output of

102
00:06:43,276 --> 00:06:43,882
that code.

103
00:06:43,882 --> 00:06:46,856
These are exactly the special tokens of nanochat.

104
00:06:47,856 --> 00:06:54,031
The most important one by far is assistant end, because that is how the model is going to

105
00:06:54,031 --> 00:06:56,182
tell us that it's done talking.

106
00:06:56,182 --> 00:06:59,581
Remember the base model that just went on and on?

107
00:06:59,581 --> 00:07:01,108
This token is the fix.

108
00:07:01,108 --> 00:07:07,075
When we sample from the model and it emits assistant end, we stop, and it's the user's

109
00:07:07,075 --> 00:07:07,422
turn.

110
00:07:07,422 --> 00:07:11,969
Now here's the function that renders a conversation into tokens.

111
00:07:11,969 --> 00:07:18,009
It's called render_conversation, and it's very simple, but there's one very important

112
00:07:18,009 --> 00:07:18,649
subtlety.

113
00:07:18,649 --> 00:07:24,830
Besides the token ids, it also returns a mask, and the mask is one for every token that

114
00:07:24,830 --> 00:07:30,018
the assistant should be trained to produce, and zero for everything else.

115
00:07:31,018 --> 00:07:34,227
So we start with the beginning of sequence token.

116
00:07:34,227 --> 00:07:36,323
Then we go through the messages.

117
00:07:36,323 --> 00:07:42,218
For a user message, we add user start, the tokens of the content, and user end, and all of

118
00:07:42,218 --> 00:07:43,856
these get a mask of zero.

119
00:07:43,856 --> 00:07:49,751
For an assistant message, we add assistant start with a mask of zero, and then the content

120
00:07:49,751 --> 00:07:55,646
with a mask of one, and then assistant end, also with a mask of one, because the assistant

121
00:07:55,646 --> 00:07:57,349
has to learn when to stop.

122
00:07:57,349 --> 00:08:03,086
The content of an assistant message can also be a list of parts, and that's for tool use.

123
00:08:03,086 --> 00:08:08,566
A python part is the assistant writing some code, so it's wrapped in python start and

124
00:08:08,566 --> 00:08:14,175
python end, and we do train on it, because at test time the assistant has to write that

125
00:08:14,175 --> 00:08:14,497
code.

126
00:08:14,497 --> 00:08:19,977
But the python output part is wrapped in output start and output end, and that one is

127
00:08:19,977 --> 00:08:25,650
masked out, because at test time this text is going to come from the python interpreter,

128
00:08:25,650 --> 00:08:26,874
not from the model.

129
00:08:26,874 --> 00:08:32,548
There's no point in training the model to predict the output of python, in fact it would

130
00:08:32,548 --> 00:08:37,447
be actively bad, it would be training the model to hallucinate tool outputs.

131
00:08:37,447 --> 00:08:38,667
Let's look at it.

132
00:08:38,667 --> 00:08:44,769
I have a little helper to visualize the tokens, where green means trained on, and red

133
00:08:44,769 --> 00:08:46,276
means not trained on.

134
00:08:47,276 --> 00:08:50,664
So the whole conversation is thirteen tokens.

135
00:08:50,664 --> 00:08:56,686
The user part is all red, and the only green tokens are Paris and assistant end.

136
00:08:56,686 --> 00:09:01,729
So the model is only going to receive gradient on those two tokens.

137
00:09:01,729 --> 00:09:04,439
The user's question is just context.

138
00:09:04,439 --> 00:09:06,738
And why do we mask the user tokens?

139
00:09:06,738 --> 00:09:08,774
Well, think about what we want.

140
00:09:08,774 --> 00:09:14,423
We are not trying to build a model that simulates users, we're trying to build a model

141
00:09:14,423 --> 00:09:15,868
that responds to them.

142
00:09:15,868 --> 00:09:21,649
If we train on the user tokens, a part of the model's capacity is spent on learning what

143
00:09:21,649 --> 00:09:27,560
kinds of questions people ask, which is not useless, but it's not the thing we care about,

144
00:09:27,560 --> 00:09:31,107
and with smaller models every bit of capacity matters.

145
00:09:31,107 --> 00:09:36,494
The other thing to note is that the very last token here, the assistant end, means

146
00:09:36,494 --> 00:09:37,150
something.

147
00:09:37,150 --> 00:09:40,566
The model has to learn that after Paris, it is done.

148
00:09:40,566 --> 00:09:45,004
Now one more function, and this is the one we'll use at test time.

149
00:09:45,004 --> 00:09:50,721
When we want the model to respond, we render everything except for the last assistant

150
00:09:50,721 --> 00:09:53,679
message, and then we append assistant start.

151
00:09:53,679 --> 00:09:55,159
And that's the prompt.

152
00:09:55,159 --> 00:10:00,471
The model then takes it from there, and we sample until it emits assistant end.

153
00:10:01,471 --> 00:10:04,733
And by the way, this format is a design choice.

154
00:10:04,733 --> 00:10:10,979
OpenAI's ChatML uses tokens like im start and im end, Llama has its own format, and so on,

155
00:10:10,979 --> 00:10:13,825
but they all do basically the same thing.

156
00:10:13,825 --> 00:10:18,683
What matters is that you're consistent between training and test time.

157
00:10:18,683 --> 00:10:24,929
If you render a conversation one way during training, and a slightly different way at test

158
00:10:24,929 --> 00:10:29,370
time, even a single space different, things can go subtly wrong.

159
00:10:30,870 --> 00:10:33,606
Okay so now we have a problem.

160
00:10:33,606 --> 00:10:40,627
GPT-2's vocabulary has 50,257 tokens and none of them are our special tokens.

161
00:10:40,627 --> 00:10:48,560
The only special token in GPT-2 is end of text, which has id 50,256, and we're going to

162
00:10:48,560 --> 00:10:52,936
use that one as our beginning of sequence token.

163
00:10:52,936 --> 00:10:55,033
But we need eight more.

164
00:10:55,033 --> 00:10:58,647
Now here's where something fun comes in.

165
00:10:58,647 --> 00:11:06,778
If you remember the GPT-2 reproduction video, at some point we changed the vocab size from

166
00:11:06,778 --> 00:11:14,638
50,257 to 50,304, because 50,304 is a nice number, it's divisible by 128 and so on, and

167
00:11:14,638 --> 00:11:17,077
the GPU kernels run faster.

168
00:11:17,077 --> 00:11:24,847
And we said those extra 47 tokens are never used, the model just learns to drive their

169
00:11:24,847 --> 00:11:26,835
probabilities to zero.

170
00:11:26,835 --> 00:11:29,816
Well, today we actually use them.

171
00:11:29,816 --> 00:11:37,044
Our special tokens get the ids 50,257 through 50,264, right inside that padding.

172
00:11:37,044 --> 00:11:42,916
So in the tokenizer we just tell tiktoken about these new tokens.

173
00:11:43,916 --> 00:11:49,676
And in the model, when we load the OpenAI weights, we copy the 50,257 rows of the

174
00:11:49,676 --> 00:11:55,791
embedding table that GPT-2 had, and then we have to decide what to do with all the new

175
00:11:55,791 --> 00:11:56,147
rows.

176
00:11:56,147 --> 00:12:01,125
Here I've initialized them to the average embedding of all the tokens.

177
00:12:01,125 --> 00:12:03,400
That's a reasonable thing to do.

178
00:12:03,400 --> 00:12:09,587
It's a neutral vector that sort of lives in the right part of the space, and it doesn't

179
00:12:09,587 --> 00:12:10,796
blow anything up.

180
00:12:11,796 --> 00:12:17,601
But now, and this is something that Karpathy really drills into you in the makemore

181
00:12:17,601 --> 00:12:23,197
videos, let's sanity check the loss at initialization, before we train anything.

182
00:12:23,197 --> 00:12:24,806
What loss do we expect?

183
00:12:24,806 --> 00:12:31,031
Well, if the model had no idea and was spreading its probability uniformly over the whole

184
00:12:31,031 --> 00:12:36,696
vocabulary, the loss would be the negative log of one over 50,304, so about 10.8.

185
00:12:36,696 --> 00:12:41,732
Let's look at the loss of every single token of our little conversation.

186
00:12:42,732 --> 00:12:44,952
Okay so this is very interesting.

187
00:12:44,952 --> 00:12:47,373
The normal English tokens look fine.

188
00:12:47,373 --> 00:12:52,215
The model is very confident about the word "is" after "what", and so on.

189
00:12:52,215 --> 00:12:54,300
But look at the special tokens.

190
00:12:54,300 --> 00:13:00,152
They all have a loss of around 15, which is way worse than the 10.8 of a uniform guess.

191
00:13:00,152 --> 00:13:02,977
The model is confidently wrong about them.

192
00:13:02,977 --> 00:13:09,030
And that's because their embeddings are the average embedding, and because the weights are

193
00:13:09,030 --> 00:13:14,612
tied, the logit of a special token is the dot product of the hidden state with that

194
00:13:14,612 --> 00:13:19,118
average vector, and in GPT-2 that happens to be a pretty low logit.

195
00:13:19,118 --> 00:13:25,037
So in the very beginning of finetuning, the loss is going to be dominated by the special

196
00:13:25,037 --> 00:13:30,821
tokens, and a lot of the early optimization is just going to be spent on learning that

197
00:13:30,821 --> 00:13:32,099
these tokens exist.

198
00:13:32,099 --> 00:13:34,318
We'll see that in the loss curve.

199
00:13:34,318 --> 00:13:39,026
It's sort of the hockey stick from the makemore videos all over again.

200
00:13:39,026 --> 00:13:44,605
By the way, in nanochat the special tokens are part of the tokenizer from the very

201
00:13:44,605 --> 00:13:47,327
beginning, so this surgery isn't needed.

202
00:13:47,327 --> 00:13:53,450
But they are never seen during pretraining, except for the beginning of sequence token, so

203
00:13:53,450 --> 00:13:55,559
they're in a similar situation.

204
00:13:55,559 --> 00:14:00,185
Their embeddings exist, but the model has never had to predict them.

205
00:14:00,185 --> 00:14:06,573
Exercise: Can you come up with a better initialization for the new embedding rows, one

206
00:14:06,573 --> 00:14:12,664
that gives a loss of about 10.8 or lower at initialization for the special tokens?

207
00:14:12,664 --> 00:14:19,201
What happens if you initialize them to the embedding of a related token, for example the

208
00:14:19,201 --> 00:14:21,875
embedding of a newline for user end?

209
00:14:21,875 --> 00:14:24,401
Pause the video and give it a try.

210
00:14:27,901 --> 00:14:29,509
Okay, now we need data.

211
00:14:29,509 --> 00:14:31,116
A lot of conversations.

212
00:14:31,116 --> 00:14:36,989
And in nanochat, the SFT data is a mixture of several datasets, and each one of them

213
00:14:36,989 --> 00:14:39,645
teaches the model something different.

214
00:14:39,645 --> 00:14:42,162
So let's go through them one by one.

215
00:14:42,162 --> 00:14:48,243
In nanochat these live in a tasks directory, and I've put them all in one tasks.py file

216
00:14:48,243 --> 00:14:48,593
here.

217
00:14:48,593 --> 00:14:54,605
A task is basically just a list of conversations, plus optionally a way of grading the

218
00:14:54,605 --> 00:14:55,094
answer.

219
00:14:55,094 --> 00:14:58,629
The first one is SmolTalk, from HuggingFace.

220
00:14:58,629 --> 00:15:05,780
This is 460,000 general conversations, and this is where most of the "personality" of the

221
00:15:05,780 --> 00:15:07,467
assistant comes from.

222
00:15:07,467 --> 00:15:08,913
Let's look at one.

223
00:15:09,913 --> 00:15:16,000
So these conversations were mostly generated synthetically, by bigger language models, and

224
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:16,947
then filtered.

225
00:15:16,947 --> 00:15:22,492
And I think it's worth pausing on this, because it's a really important point that

226
00:15:22,492 --> 00:15:25,130
Karpathy makes in his talks about LLMs.

227
00:15:25,130 --> 00:15:28,444
When you talk to ChatGPT, who are you talking to?

228
00:15:28,444 --> 00:15:34,531
Well, the answer is that you're talking to a statistical simulation of the data in the SFT

229
00:15:34,531 --> 00:15:34,936
stage.

230
00:15:34,936 --> 00:15:40,820
Originally that data was written by human labelers following long labeling instructions

231
00:15:40,820 --> 00:15:45,081
from a company, about how to be helpful, truthful and harmless.

232
00:15:45,081 --> 00:15:48,192
Nowadays a lot of it is written by other LLMs.

233
00:15:48,192 --> 00:15:53,805
But either way, the persona, the tone, the length of the answers, the little bullet

234
00:15:53,805 --> 00:15:57,255
points, all of that comes from imitating this data.

235
00:15:57,255 --> 00:16:02,733
The knowledge comes from pretraining, but the personality is downloaded from SFT.

236
00:16:02,733 --> 00:16:08,762
The second one is MMLU, which is multiple choice questions across 57 subjects.

237
00:16:08,762 --> 00:16:15,642
And this one teaches the model the format of multiple choice, which is how we're going to

238
00:16:15,642 --> 00:16:17,033
evaluate it later.

239
00:16:17,033 --> 00:16:17,884
Let's look.

240
00:16:18,884 --> 00:16:24,872
So the user gives a question and some choices, and the assistant responds with a single

241
00:16:24,872 --> 00:16:25,353
letter.

242
00:16:25,353 --> 00:16:31,410
Now, the way this prompt is rendered actually hides two subtle details that matter a lot

243
00:16:31,410 --> 00:16:32,580
for small models.

244
00:16:32,580 --> 00:16:34,714
And I'll show you the function.

245
00:16:35,714 --> 00:16:40,368
The first detail is that the letter comes after the choice, not before it.

246
00:16:40,368 --> 00:16:46,028
Big models don't care much, but for small models, having the letter right after the choice

247
00:16:46,028 --> 00:16:48,921
text helps them bind the letter to the choice.

248
00:16:48,921 --> 00:16:54,142
The second detail is that there is no space between the equals sign and the letter.

249
00:16:54,142 --> 00:16:56,406
And that's because of the tokenizer.

250
00:16:56,406 --> 00:16:57,160
Let's check.

251
00:16:58,160 --> 00:17:03,009
The token "space A" is a completely different token from the token "A".

252
00:17:03,009 --> 00:17:08,882
They have nothing to do with each other as far as the model is concerned, they're just

253
00:17:08,882 --> 00:17:10,179
different integers.

254
00:17:10,179 --> 00:17:15,096
And the assistant's answer is going to be the token "A" without a space.

255
00:17:15,096 --> 00:17:20,969
So in the prompt we want the exact same token to appear, so that the model can sort of

256
00:17:20,969 --> 00:17:22,471
just point back at it.

257
00:17:22,471 --> 00:17:28,548
If you remember the tokenization video, a lot of the weirdness of LLMs traces back to the

258
00:17:28,548 --> 00:17:31,963
tokenizer, and this is one of those little things.

259
00:17:31,963 --> 00:17:35,466
The third one is GSM8K, grade school math.

260
00:17:35,466 --> 00:17:41,639
And this one is really interesting because the solutions use a calculator.

261
00:17:41,639 --> 00:17:44,476
Let's look at a raw example first.

262
00:17:45,476 --> 00:17:49,270
So you see these little things in double angle brackets?

263
00:17:49,270 --> 00:17:53,404
Those are the calculator annotations of the original dataset.

264
00:17:53,404 --> 00:17:59,096
So two times twelve equals twenty four is written as double angle bracket, two times

265
00:17:59,096 --> 00:18:02,349
twelve equals twenty four, double angle bracket.

266
00:18:02,349 --> 00:18:08,312
And what we do in our task is we parse these out, and turn them into a python tool call,

267
00:18:08,312 --> 00:18:10,684
followed by the output of the tool.

268
00:18:12,684 --> 00:18:14,042
So now look at this.

269
00:18:14,042 --> 00:18:19,747
The model is trained to write "2 times 12", and then python end, and then the output

270
00:18:19,747 --> 00:18:21,377
tokens, the 24, are red.

271
00:18:21,377 --> 00:18:27,286
They're not trained on, because at test time they will be filled in by our python tool.

272
00:18:27,286 --> 00:18:30,682
And then the model copies that 24 into its answer.

273
00:18:30,682 --> 00:18:36,727
And at the very end, the answer is given after four hash signs, and that's what we'll use

274
00:18:36,727 --> 00:18:37,542
to grade it.

275
00:18:37,542 --> 00:18:40,938
This is the simplest possible version of tool use.

276
00:18:40,938 --> 00:18:46,711
The model learns to offload arithmetic, which it is bad at, to a calculator, which is

277
00:18:46,711 --> 00:18:47,662
perfect at it.

278
00:18:47,662 --> 00:18:50,056
And the fourth one is my favorite.

279
00:18:50,056 --> 00:18:56,324
It's the SpellingBee task, which was added to nanochat so that it could answer the famous

280
00:18:56,324 --> 00:18:59,634
question, how many r's are there in strawberry.

281
00:18:59,634 --> 00:19:01,888
Now why is this hard for an LLM?

282
00:19:01,888 --> 00:19:05,268
Well, let's look at how strawberry is tokenized.

283
00:19:06,268 --> 00:19:08,828
So "space strawberry" is a single token.

284
00:19:08,828 --> 00:19:10,236
It's just one integer.

285
00:19:10,236 --> 00:19:12,348
The model never sees the letters.

286
00:19:12,348 --> 00:19:17,979
It has to somehow memorize, for every one of its fifty thousand tokens, what letters are

287
00:19:17,979 --> 00:19:22,586
inside of it, and then count them in its head, in a single forward pass.

288
00:19:22,586 --> 00:19:23,802
That's really hard.

289
00:19:23,802 --> 00:19:28,986
And without a space in front, it's three tokens, "st", "raw", "berry", which is a

290
00:19:28,986 --> 00:19:31,738
completely different set of integers again.

291
00:19:31,738 --> 00:19:34,531
So SpellingBee is a synthetic task.

292
00:19:34,531 --> 00:19:41,475
We generate as many examples as we want, by taking a random word from a list of 370,000

293
00:19:41,475 --> 00:19:47,542
English words and a random letter, and then we write out the ideal solution.

294
00:19:47,542 --> 00:19:48,978
Let's look at one.

295
00:19:49,978 --> 00:19:55,725
So the ideal answer first spells the word out letter by letter, and spelling is something

296
00:19:55,725 --> 00:19:57,017
the model can learn.

297
00:19:57,017 --> 00:20:02,828
Then it goes through the letters one at a time and keeps a running count, so that it never

298
00:20:02,828 --> 00:20:05,992
has to do too much computation in a single token.

299
00:20:05,992 --> 00:20:11,674
And then it double checks the answer with python, using the count method of strings, and

300
00:20:11,674 --> 00:20:13,353
then gives a final answer.

301
00:20:13,353 --> 00:20:19,035
This is a really nice example of how you have to think about the cognition of the model.

302
00:20:19,035 --> 00:20:24,588
You want to spread the computation across many tokens, because every token only gets a

303
00:20:24,588 --> 00:20:29,044
fixed amount of compute, a single forward pass through twelve layers.

304
00:20:29,044 --> 00:20:34,855
If you ask the model to blurt out the answer immediately, you're asking it to do the whole

305
00:20:34,855 --> 00:20:38,148
computation in one forward pass, and it just can't.

306
00:20:38,148 --> 00:20:40,731
And then we mix all of these together.

307
00:20:40,731 --> 00:20:46,779
In nanochat this is a TaskMixture, which just takes a list of tasks, builds the full list

308
00:20:46,779 --> 00:20:50,652
of all their examples, and shuffles it with a fixed seed.

309
00:20:50,652 --> 00:20:56,225
And if you want to oversample something, you can just pass the same task in twice.

310
00:20:58,225 --> 00:21:01,367
Exercise: Write your own synthetic task.

311
00:21:01,367 --> 00:21:08,201
For example, a task that teaches the model its own name and who made it, or a task that

312
00:21:08,201 --> 00:21:09,458
reverses a word.

313
00:21:09,458 --> 00:21:15,664
Generate the ideal response programmatically, and think about how to spread the

314
00:21:15,664 --> 00:21:17,706
computation across tokens.

315
00:21:17,706 --> 00:21:20,377
Pause the video and give it a try.

316
00:21:23,877 --> 00:21:29,221
Okay, now we need to batch these conversations up so that we can train on them.

317
00:21:29,221 --> 00:21:32,941
And this is actually more interesting than you'd think.

318
00:21:32,941 --> 00:21:34,973
In pretraining, this was easy.

319
00:21:34,973 --> 00:21:40,864
We had one infinite stream of text, and we just chopped it up into rows of 1024 tokens.

320
00:21:40,864 --> 00:21:43,641
If a document got cut in half, who cares.

321
00:21:43,641 --> 00:21:45,808
But conversations are different.

322
00:21:45,808 --> 00:21:47,501
A conversation is a unit.

323
00:21:47,501 --> 00:21:53,527
If you chop a conversation in half, the second half starts with no user question, and the

324
00:21:53,527 --> 00:21:59,080
first half has no answer, so we really want every conversation to be in one piece.

325
00:21:59,080 --> 00:22:05,039
And also, every row should start at the beginning of a conversation, with a beginning of

326
00:22:05,039 --> 00:22:09,983
sequence token, because that's what it's going to look like at test time.

327
00:22:09,983 --> 00:22:15,309
So the naive approach is to put one conversation per row and pad the rest.

328
00:22:15,309 --> 00:22:17,324
But conversations are short.

329
00:22:17,324 --> 00:22:23,226
Most of them are a few hundred tokens, so you'd be wasting most of your compute on

330
00:22:23,226 --> 00:22:23,802
padding.

331
00:22:23,802 --> 00:22:25,880
The better approach is packing.

332
00:22:25,880 --> 00:22:29,835
We put as many whole conversations into each row as we can.

333
00:22:29,835 --> 00:22:33,187
And nanochat uses a "best fit" algorithm for this.

334
00:22:33,187 --> 00:22:38,752
We keep a buffer of, say, 100 conversations, and for each row, we keep grabbing the

335
00:22:38,752 --> 00:22:43,713
longest conversation in the buffer that still fits in the remaining space.

336
00:22:43,713 --> 00:22:49,009
When nothing fits anymore, we pad the rest of the row, and we never, ever crop.

337
00:22:49,009 --> 00:22:50,551
Let's look at the code.

338
00:22:51,551 --> 00:22:55,045
So we build up each row, and a mask row alongside it.

339
00:22:55,045 --> 00:22:57,155
The padding gets a mask of zero.

340
00:22:57,155 --> 00:22:59,990
And then at the end, here's the crux of it.

341
00:22:59,990 --> 00:23:05,923
The inputs are the row without its last token, and the targets are the row shifted by one,

342
00:23:05,923 --> 00:23:07,769
exactly like in pretraining.

343
00:23:07,769 --> 00:23:12,187
And then wherever the mask is zero, we set the target to minus one.

344
00:23:12,187 --> 00:23:16,999
And minus one is the ignore index of the cross entropy loss in our model.

345
00:23:16,999 --> 00:23:20,625
So those positions simply don't contribute to the loss.

346
00:23:21,625 --> 00:23:25,021
Let's try it out and see how efficient this is.

347
00:23:26,021 --> 00:23:30,318
So only about 40 percent of the tokens are actually trained on.

348
00:23:30,318 --> 00:23:33,318
The rest is user prompts and special tokens.

349
00:23:33,318 --> 00:23:39,252
And the padding is only about 3 percent, and that number even includes the beginning of

350
00:23:39,252 --> 00:23:44,707
sequence token of every conversation, so best fit packing wastes almost nothing.

351
00:23:44,707 --> 00:23:50,641
But it does mean that in SFT, a batch of a given size carries a lot less signal than in

352
00:23:50,641 --> 00:23:54,051
pretraining, where every single token is a target.

353
00:23:54,051 --> 00:23:59,732
Now I want to tell you about a bug that I hit while writing this, because it's really

354
00:23:59,732 --> 00:24:00,534
instructive.

355
00:24:00,534 --> 00:24:06,349
In my first version, I rendered every conversation with a maximum length, and if it was

356
00:24:06,349 --> 00:24:08,956
longer than a row, I just truncated it.

357
00:24:08,956 --> 00:24:10,427
Seems harmless, right?

358
00:24:10,427 --> 00:24:16,242
But when I ran a small test, the loss came out as nan, and the number of trained tokens

359
00:24:16,242 --> 00:24:16,843
was zero.

360
00:24:16,843 --> 00:24:17,979
So what happened?

361
00:24:17,979 --> 00:24:23,995
Well, MMLU's auxiliary train set has a lot of really long reading comprehension questions,

362
00:24:23,995 --> 00:24:27,337
and the answer is a single letter at the very end.

363
00:24:27,337 --> 00:24:33,219
If you truncate that, you cut off the answer, and you're left with a conversation that's

364
00:24:33,219 --> 00:24:35,759
all prompt and no green tokens at all.

365
00:24:35,759 --> 00:24:41,240
And the best fit algorithm loves these, because they are exactly as long as a row!

366
00:24:41,240 --> 00:24:46,721
So it was filling entire batches with conversations that have nothing to train on.

367
00:24:46,721 --> 00:24:51,266
And the mean over zero tokens is zero divided by zero, which is nan.

368
00:24:51,266 --> 00:24:57,215
The fix is the one line you see here: if a conversation doesn't fit in a row, we skip it.

369
00:24:57,215 --> 00:24:59,487
That's what "we never crop" means.

370
00:24:59,487 --> 00:25:06,500
Exercise: Measure how much padding you'd have with the naive one-conversation-per-row

371
00:25:06,500 --> 00:25:09,717
approach, compared to best fit packing.

372
00:25:09,717 --> 00:25:12,522
Pause the video and give it a try.

373
00:25:16,022 --> 00:25:22,135
Okay so now we can write the training loop, and the punchline is that SFT is literally

374
00:25:22,135 --> 00:25:25,404
just pretraining, with a different dataloader.

375
00:25:25,404 --> 00:25:30,308
It's the same model, the same cross entropy loss, the same optimizer.

376
00:25:30,308 --> 00:25:34,075
The only difference is the minus ones in the targets.

377
00:25:34,075 --> 00:25:34,857
Let's look.

378
00:25:35,857 --> 00:25:39,508
The optimizer is AdamW, the same as in the GPT-2 video.

379
00:25:39,508 --> 00:25:45,417
The learning rate is constant for the first half of training, and then decays linearly to

380
00:25:45,417 --> 00:25:45,749
zero.

381
00:25:45,749 --> 00:25:48,338
That's the schedule that nanochat uses.

382
00:25:48,338 --> 00:25:51,458
There's no warmup, and there's no weight decay.

383
00:25:51,458 --> 00:25:56,969
The learning rate is a lot lower than in pretraining, we're not trying to learn the

384
00:25:56,969 --> 00:26:02,811
internet again, we're just gently nudging the statistics of a model that already knows a

385
00:26:02,811 --> 00:26:03,076
lot.

386
00:26:03,076 --> 00:26:08,209
In nanochat, the default is to do one full epoch over the whole mixture.

387
00:26:08,209 --> 00:26:14,483
Here, on a GPU, that's around 600,000 conversations, and it takes something like half an

388
00:26:14,483 --> 00:26:16,051
hour on a single A100.

389
00:26:16,051 --> 00:26:20,757
But I want to show you that you don't need a GPU to see this work.

390
00:26:20,757 --> 00:26:26,959
So I'm going to run a much smaller version, on a plain four core CPU: 600 steps of four

391
00:26:26,959 --> 00:26:28,670
rows of 512 tokens each.

392
00:26:28,670 --> 00:26:31,236
That takes about an hour and a half.

393
00:26:33,736 --> 00:26:36,012
So look at the very first step.

394
00:26:36,012 --> 00:26:42,544
The loss is 11.9, and that's the special tokens we talked about, the model is confidently

395
00:26:42,544 --> 00:26:43,792
wrong about them.

396
00:26:43,792 --> 00:26:47,682
But also look at the number of trained tokens: eight.

397
00:26:47,682 --> 00:26:53,922
In the entire first batch of four rows of 512 tokens, there were only eight tokens to

398
00:26:53,922 --> 00:26:54,582
train on.

399
00:26:54,582 --> 00:26:57,665
And that's the best fit algorithm at work.

400
00:26:57,665 --> 00:27:03,610
At the start, the longest conversations in the buffer are those long MMLU reading

401
00:27:03,610 --> 00:27:10,143
comprehension questions, so each row gets one of them, and each one has exactly two green

402
00:27:10,143 --> 00:27:13,153
tokens, the letter and the assistant end.

403
00:27:13,153 --> 00:27:17,190
Then step two has 428 trained tokens and a loss of 2.6.

404
00:27:17,190 --> 00:27:23,282
So the training loss per step is really noisy, because every batch has a completely

405
00:27:23,282 --> 00:27:28,787
different composition, and it's much better to look at the validation loss.

406
00:27:28,787 --> 00:27:33,191
And the validation loss goes down nicely, from 2.73 to 1.34.

407
00:27:33,191 --> 00:27:35,084
Okay, so now the fun part.

408
00:27:35,084 --> 00:27:36,321
Let's talk to it.

409
00:27:36,321 --> 00:27:42,290
I'm going to write a little chat function, that renders a conversation, primes the

410
00:27:42,290 --> 00:27:46,148
assistant, and samples until the assistant end token.

411
00:27:47,148 --> 00:27:47,624
"Hello!

412
00:27:47,624 --> 00:27:50,821
How can I help you today?" And then it stopped.

413
00:27:50,821 --> 00:27:54,223
It emitted the assistant end token, all by itself.

414
00:27:54,223 --> 00:28:00,074
Compare that to the base model, which would have just kept going forever, writing both

415
00:28:00,074 --> 00:28:01,842
sides of the conversation.

416
00:28:01,842 --> 00:28:03,883
So that's the format, learned.

417
00:28:03,883 --> 00:28:06,060
Let's ask it about France again.

418
00:28:07,060 --> 00:28:11,911
So it answers directly now, as an assistant, "The capital of France is Paris".

419
00:28:11,911 --> 00:28:13,963
But then it gets stuck in a loop.

420
00:28:13,963 --> 00:28:19,436
And this is a very common failure of greedy decoding with small models: once it has said

421
00:28:19,436 --> 00:28:24,970
"the capital of France" a couple of times, the most likely next thing is to say it again,

422
00:28:24,970 --> 00:28:28,018
and it falls into a rut that it can't get out of.

423
00:28:28,018 --> 00:28:30,132
Sampling with a temperature helps.

424
00:28:30,132 --> 00:28:33,366
But let me show you something even more interesting.

425
00:28:34,366 --> 00:28:34,566
Ha!

426
00:28:34,566 --> 00:28:35,434
Look at this.

427
00:28:35,434 --> 00:28:38,371
"We are asked to find the capital of France.

428
00:28:38,371 --> 00:28:44,246
Let me try a manual approach first." And then it spells France, badly, counts the letter

429
00:28:44,246 --> 00:28:46,650
f, and calls python to double check.

430
00:28:46,650 --> 00:28:50,855
It's running the SpellingBee procedure on a geography question!

431
00:28:50,855 --> 00:28:52,458
So what's going on here?

432
00:28:52,458 --> 00:28:58,399
Well, in this little CPU run, SpellingBee is twenty thousand of the 75,000 conversations,

433
00:28:58,399 --> 00:29:01,804
so more than a quarter of everything the model saw.

434
00:29:01,804 --> 00:29:07,812
And a lot of the SpellingBee questions are short questions like "What is the count of e in

435
00:29:07,812 --> 00:29:08,547
some word".

436
00:29:08,547 --> 00:29:14,355
So "What is the capital of France" sort of looks like one of those, on the surface, and

437
00:29:14,355 --> 00:29:17,426
the model pattern matches on the surface form.

438
00:29:17,426 --> 00:29:23,367
If I sample answers to thirty real questions from the SmolTalk test set, only two of them

439
00:29:23,367 --> 00:29:26,839
start like this, so it's mostly the short questions.

440
00:29:26,839 --> 00:29:30,377
But it's a really good lesson about the data mixture.

441
00:29:30,377 --> 00:29:36,119
Every dataset you put in there shapes the behavior of the model, in ways that leak out

442
00:29:36,119 --> 00:29:37,854
beyond the dataset itself.

443
00:29:37,854 --> 00:29:43,529
In fact, when SpellingBee was added to nanochat, Karpathy noted in the commit that it

444
00:29:43,529 --> 00:29:47,201
probably uses way too many of these synthetic examples.

445
00:29:47,201 --> 00:29:52,942
On a GPU, with a full epoch, SmolTalk dominates the mixture and this mostly goes away.

446
00:29:53,942 --> 00:29:58,178
And this one is kind of hilarious, meteoroids and molten metal.

447
00:29:58,178 --> 00:30:01,070
So let's be honest about what we have here.

448
00:30:01,070 --> 00:30:06,853
This is a 124 million parameter model from 2019, finetuned for an hour and a half on a

449
00:30:06,853 --> 00:30:07,122
CPU.

450
00:30:07,122 --> 00:30:12,972
It doesn't really know why the sky is blue, and it's very happy to make things up, with

451
00:30:12,972 --> 00:30:14,115
total confidence.

452
00:30:14,115 --> 00:30:19,898
The nanochat README says that talking to its speedrun model is a bit like talking to a

453
00:30:19,898 --> 00:30:22,722
kindergartener, and we're well below that.

454
00:30:22,722 --> 00:30:28,639
But the format is there, the turn taking is there, and as we'll see, on the narrow tasks

455
00:30:28,639 --> 00:30:32,943
that we specifically trained for, it can actually do quite well.

456
00:30:32,943 --> 00:30:40,087
Exercise: Re-run SFT with fewer SpellingBee examples, say five thousand instead of twenty

457
00:30:40,087 --> 00:30:47,312
thousand, and measure how often the model answers ordinary questions with "We are asked to

458
00:30:47,312 --> 00:30:47,794
find".

459
00:30:47,794 --> 00:30:51,085
What happens to its SpellingBee accuracy?

460
00:30:51,085 --> 00:30:53,815
Pause the video and give it a try.

461
00:30:57,315 --> 00:31:03,368
Okay, so now let's talk about the Engine, which is what we've been using to sample from

462
00:31:03,368 --> 00:31:04,063
the model.

463
00:31:04,063 --> 00:31:06,638
In nanochat, the engine has two jobs.

464
00:31:06,638 --> 00:31:11,578
The first is to make sampling fast, and the second is to run the tools.

465
00:31:11,578 --> 00:31:14,122
So first, why is naive sampling slow?

466
00:31:14,122 --> 00:31:17,835
Well, think about what we do in a naive sampling loop.

467
00:31:17,835 --> 00:31:23,748
We take the whole sequence, run it through the transformer, get the logits at the last

468
00:31:23,748 --> 00:31:29,249
position, sample a token, append it, and then run the whole sequence through the

469
00:31:29,249 --> 00:31:30,486
transformer again.

470
00:31:30,486 --> 00:31:35,024
But for all the tokens that we already processed, nothing changed!

471
00:31:35,024 --> 00:31:41,144
Their keys and values in every attention layer are exactly the same as last time, because

472
00:31:41,144 --> 00:31:46,576
of the causal mask, a token can never be affected by tokens that come after it.

473
00:31:46,576 --> 00:31:52,352
So we're recomputing the same things over and over, and for long sequences this gets

474
00:31:52,352 --> 00:31:53,452
really wasteful.

475
00:31:53,452 --> 00:31:55,311
The fix is the KV cache.

476
00:31:55,311 --> 00:32:02,205
We keep the keys and values of every layer around, and when a new token comes in, we only

477
00:32:02,205 --> 00:32:09,021
compute the query, key and value for that one token, append the new key and value to the

478
00:32:09,021 --> 00:32:14,443
cache, and let the single new query attend to everything in the cache.

479
00:32:14,443 --> 00:32:17,077
Here's the KV cache in our gpt.py.

480
00:32:17,077 --> 00:32:17,929
It is tiny.

481
00:32:19,929 --> 00:32:25,556
So in the attention, if there's a cache, we append the new keys and values and get back

482
00:32:25,556 --> 00:32:27,173
the full keys and values.

483
00:32:27,173 --> 00:32:32,865
And one subtle thing: if we're feeding in a single token, we don't want a causal mask at

484
00:32:32,865 --> 00:32:37,717
all, because that one new token is allowed to look at everything before it.

485
00:32:37,717 --> 00:32:42,956
And in the model's forward, the positions have to start where the cache left off,

486
00:32:42,956 --> 00:32:47,743
otherwise the new token would get the position embedding of position zero.

487
00:32:47,743 --> 00:32:53,500
Let's make sure the cache actually gives exactly the same answer, and see how much faster

488
00:32:53,500 --> 00:32:53,888
it is.

489
00:32:54,888 --> 00:33:01,289
So with the KV cache, generating these 100 tokens is about four times faster on my CPU,

490
00:33:01,289 --> 00:33:05,115
and the result is exactly the same, token for token.

491
00:33:05,115 --> 00:33:11,295
And that gap only grows with length, because the naive version reprocesses the whole

492
00:33:11,295 --> 00:33:17,844
sequence for every new token, so the total work grows quadratically, while with the cache

493
00:33:17,844 --> 00:33:20,566
every new token costs about the same.

494
00:33:20,566 --> 00:33:26,413
The other trick in the engine is for when we want many samples for the same prompt, which

495
00:33:26,413 --> 00:33:30,157
is going to be very important for reinforcement learning.

496
00:33:30,157 --> 00:33:35,741
We process the prompt once, with a batch size of one, and this is called the prefill.

497
00:33:35,741 --> 00:33:41,259
And then we just copy the cache for every sample, and then decode all the samples in

498
00:33:41,259 --> 00:33:42,770
parallel, as one batch.

499
00:33:42,770 --> 00:33:45,929
Now the second job of the engine is tool use.

500
00:33:45,929 --> 00:33:48,667
And this is where it gets kind of cute.

501
00:33:48,667 --> 00:33:50,633
Let's look at the main loop.

502
00:33:51,633 --> 00:33:55,839
Exercise: Before I explain it, pause and think about it.

503
00:33:55,839 --> 00:33:59,068
The model is sampling tokens one at a time.

504
00:33:59,068 --> 00:34:04,400
At some point it emits python start, then some tokens, then python end.

505
00:34:04,400 --> 00:34:10,709
What exactly should the engine do at that point, and what should the model see next?

506
00:34:10,709 --> 00:34:13,262
Pause the video and give it a try.

507
00:34:15,262 --> 00:34:18,002
So every row keeps a little bit of state.

508
00:34:18,002 --> 00:34:23,682
When the model samples python start, we start recording the tokens of the expression.

509
00:34:23,682 --> 00:34:29,696
When it samples python end, we decode the expression, and evaluate it with our calculator.

510
00:34:29,696 --> 00:34:35,576
And then here's the trick: we put output start, the tokens of the result, and output end

511
00:34:35,576 --> 00:34:37,580
into a queue of forced tokens.

512
00:34:37,580 --> 00:34:43,394
And on the next steps, instead of using what the model sampled, we pop tokens from that

513
00:34:43,394 --> 00:34:43,795
queue.

514
00:34:43,795 --> 00:34:49,809
So the model sees the result of the tool appear in its own context, as if it wrote it, and

515
00:34:49,809 --> 00:34:52,348
then it continues sampling from there.

516
00:34:52,348 --> 00:34:58,228
And we also return a mask, one for sampled tokens and zero for forced tokens, and you'll

517
00:34:58,228 --> 00:35:01,369
see why that matters in reinforcement learning.

518
00:35:01,369 --> 00:35:05,034
The calculator itself is deliberately very restricted.

519
00:35:05,034 --> 00:35:10,871
We only allow numbers and arithmetic, or the count method of a string, and we evaluate

520
00:35:10,871 --> 00:35:12,296
without any builtins.

521
00:35:12,296 --> 00:35:17,998
You really don't want to give a language model an unrestricted eval on your machine.

522
00:35:17,998 --> 00:35:24,038
In nanochat it's the same story, and there's a separate, more careful sandbox for running

523
00:35:24,038 --> 00:35:25,192
real python code.

524
00:35:26,192 --> 00:35:27,868
Let's watch it in action.

525
00:35:27,868 --> 00:35:33,901
Here's a SpellingBee question from the test set, and I'm going to visualize what comes out

526
00:35:33,901 --> 00:35:39,935
of the engine, where green tokens were sampled by the model, and red tokens were forced in

527
00:35:39,935 --> 00:35:40,873
by the engine.

528
00:35:41,873 --> 00:35:46,811
So look at what happened here, because this is a really nice little example.

529
00:35:46,811 --> 00:35:49,799
The question is how many u's are in eurytherm.

530
00:35:49,799 --> 00:35:53,957
The model tries to spell it out, and it gets the spelling wrong.

531
00:35:53,957 --> 00:35:56,036
It writes e, y, u, r, and so on.

532
00:35:56,036 --> 00:36:01,884
It roughly knows which letters are in there, but not in the right order, because remember,

533
00:36:01,884 --> 00:36:04,158
it never actually sees the letters.

534
00:36:04,158 --> 00:36:10,005
Then it counts the u's in its own misspelled version, and it gets one, which happens to be

535
00:36:10,005 --> 00:36:10,395
right.

536
00:36:10,395 --> 00:36:15,982
And then it writes the python call, and in the python call it copies the word exactly,

537
00:36:15,982 --> 00:36:18,061
because copying a token is easy.

538
00:36:18,061 --> 00:36:23,778
Then the engine runs the code and forces in output start, one, output end, and those are

539
00:36:23,778 --> 00:36:26,897
the only three red tokens in the whole response.

540
00:36:26,897 --> 00:36:29,951
And Python says one, and the model reports one.

541
00:36:29,951 --> 00:36:33,329
So the tool is robust to the model's own sloppiness.

542
00:36:33,329 --> 00:36:37,357
Copying the word into python is a lot easier than spelling it.

543
00:36:38,857 --> 00:36:44,926
Okay so we have a model that seems to work, but "seems to work" is not good enough.

544
00:36:44,926 --> 00:36:46,096
We need numbers.

545
00:36:46,096 --> 00:36:52,164
And so in nanochat there's a chat_eval script, and there are basically two kinds of

546
00:36:52,164 --> 00:36:53,042
evaluations.

547
00:36:53,042 --> 00:36:56,996
The first kind is categorical, which is for multiple choice.

548
00:36:56,996 --> 00:36:58,578
And here's a neat thing.

549
00:36:58,578 --> 00:37:01,346
We don't actually have to sample anything.

550
00:37:01,346 --> 00:37:07,145
We render the question, prime the assistant, run a single forward pass, and then we look

551
00:37:07,145 --> 00:37:13,077
at the logits at the very last position, but only at the logits of the allowed letters, A,

552
00:37:13,077 --> 00:37:13,802
B, C and D.

553
00:37:13,802 --> 00:37:17,492
And whichever one is highest, that's the model's answer.

554
00:37:18,492 --> 00:37:24,201
So this is kind of generous to the model, because even if it would have said something

555
00:37:24,201 --> 00:37:27,519
else, we're forcing it to pick one of the letters.

556
00:37:27,519 --> 00:37:33,493
But that's how these evaluations are typically done, and it makes them fast and low-noise.

557
00:37:33,493 --> 00:37:37,144
And the random baseline for four choices is 25 percent.

558
00:37:37,144 --> 00:37:42,263
The second kind is generative, for things like GSM8K and SpellingBee.

559
00:37:42,263 --> 00:37:48,940
Here we actually sample a response, then extract the number after the four hash signs, and

560
00:37:48,940 --> 00:37:51,537
compare it to the reference answer.

561
00:37:52,537 --> 00:37:57,126
And then to get one number, nanochat has something called ChatCORE.

562
00:37:57,126 --> 00:38:03,086
It takes the accuracy on every task, rescales it so that zero means random guessing and

563
00:38:03,086 --> 00:38:05,963
one means perfect, and then averages them.

564
00:38:05,963 --> 00:38:11,785
So let's evaluate both the base model and our SFT model on 100 problems of each task.

565
00:38:11,785 --> 00:38:17,881
For the base model, I'm going to use the same chat format, which it has never seen, so we

566
00:38:17,881 --> 00:38:19,731
expect it to be pretty bad.

567
00:38:22,231 --> 00:38:28,599
Okay so let's be honest about these numbers, because this is exactly where evaluations

568
00:38:28,599 --> 00:38:29,784
earn their keep.

569
00:38:29,784 --> 00:38:35,560
On the multiple choice tasks, both models are basically at chance, 25 percent.

570
00:38:35,560 --> 00:38:42,076
And with only 100 problems, the noise on each of these numbers is something like plus or

571
00:38:42,076 --> 00:38:47,186
minus four or five percent, so 24 versus 29 is not a real difference.

572
00:38:47,186 --> 00:38:53,776
GPT-2 small simply doesn't have the knowledge to answer MMLU questions, and SFT can't add

573
00:38:53,776 --> 00:38:57,997
knowledge that isn't there, it can only teach the format.

574
00:38:57,997 --> 00:39:04,587
You need a much bigger and better pretrained model for these to move, which is a big part

575
00:39:04,587 --> 00:39:09,104
of why nanochat pretrains its own, much stronger base models.

576
00:39:09,104 --> 00:39:10,808
GSM8K is zero for both.

577
00:39:10,808 --> 00:39:17,176
The SFT model has learned the format of a math solution, with calculator calls and the

578
00:39:17,176 --> 00:39:22,137
four hash signs at the end, but it can't actually do the reasoning.

579
00:39:22,137 --> 00:39:25,469
But SpellingBee goes from zero to 53 percent.

580
00:39:25,469 --> 00:39:31,764
That's a narrow, procedural task, and SFT taught the model a procedure, spell, count,

581
00:39:31,764 --> 00:39:35,836
double check with python, that it can actually execute.

582
00:39:35,836 --> 00:39:41,612
So the ChatCORE goes from 0.02 to 0.11, and essentially all of that comes from

583
00:39:41,612 --> 00:39:42,501
SpellingBee.

584
00:39:42,501 --> 00:39:49,091
And 53 percent is not great, but it's also not zero, which is exactly where we want to be

585
00:39:49,091 --> 00:39:50,498
for the next stage.

586
00:39:50,498 --> 00:39:58,627
Exercise: Try evaluating the base model on MMLU with a few-shot prompt instead of the chat

587
00:39:58,627 --> 00:39:59,259
format.

588
00:39:59,259 --> 00:40:04,407
How close can you get to the SFT model just by prompting?

589
00:40:04,407 --> 00:40:07,478
Pause the video and give it a try.

590
00:40:10,978 --> 00:40:16,321
Okay, so now we get to the last stage, and I think the most exciting one.

591
00:40:16,321 --> 00:40:18,005
Reinforcement learning.

592
00:40:18,005 --> 00:40:19,776
So first, why do we need it?

593
00:40:19,776 --> 00:40:22,559
Karpathy has a really nice analogy for this.

594
00:40:22,559 --> 00:40:25,216
Think about how you learn from a textbook.

595
00:40:25,216 --> 00:40:30,656
Most of the textbook is exposition, background knowledge, and that's like pretraining.

596
00:40:30,656 --> 00:40:36,222
Then there are worked examples, where an expert shows you how to solve a problem step by

597
00:40:36,222 --> 00:40:37,930
step, and you imitate them.

598
00:40:37,930 --> 00:40:38,879
And that's SFT.

599
00:40:38,879 --> 00:40:43,054
But then at the end of every chapter, there are practice problems.

600
00:40:43,054 --> 00:40:48,494
You're given the problem, and the final answer is in the back of the book, but not the

601
00:40:48,494 --> 00:40:49,063
solution.

602
00:40:49,063 --> 00:40:54,124
And you have to try, and fail, and try again, and figure out what works for you.

603
00:40:54,124 --> 00:40:56,274
And that's reinforcement learning.

604
00:40:56,274 --> 00:40:59,217
And here's the deep reason why this matters.

605
00:40:59,217 --> 00:41:05,169
When we do SFT on a GSM8K solution, we're asking the model to imitate a human's solution.

606
00:41:05,169 --> 00:41:08,914
But the human has a very different brain from the model.

607
00:41:08,914 --> 00:41:14,866
Some step that's trivial for the human might be a huge leap for the model, a leap that it

608
00:41:14,866 --> 00:41:20,751
can't make in a single token, and some step that the human spells out carefully might be

609
00:41:20,751 --> 00:41:22,691
totally obvious to the model.

610
00:41:22,691 --> 00:41:28,710
We don't actually know what the best token sequence is for this particular model to get to

611
00:41:28,710 --> 00:41:29,846
the right answer.

612
00:41:29,846 --> 00:41:34,127
And so the idea of reinforcement learning is, let's not tell it.

613
00:41:34,127 --> 00:41:38,540
Let the model try many times, and whatever works, do more of that.

614
00:41:38,540 --> 00:41:40,520
So how do we actually do that?

615
00:41:40,520 --> 00:41:46,196
Let's say we have a question, and the model samples an answer y, and then we check the

616
00:41:46,196 --> 00:41:51,080
answer and give it a reward R, one if it's correct and zero if it's wrong.

617
00:41:51,080 --> 00:41:56,624
And what we want is to maximize the expected reward, the average reward over all the

618
00:41:56,624 --> 00:41:59,000
answers that the model might sample.

619
00:41:59,000 --> 00:42:04,346
Now, it turns out there's a really neat trick for computing the gradient of that.

620
00:42:04,346 --> 00:42:10,286
The gradient of the expected reward is equal to the expected value of the reward times the

621
00:42:10,286 --> 00:42:13,322
gradient of the log probability of the sample.

622
00:42:13,322 --> 00:42:18,997
This is called the policy gradient, or the REINFORCE trick, and it comes from the fact

623
00:42:18,997 --> 00:42:23,287
that the gradient of p is equal to p times the gradient of log p.

624
00:42:23,287 --> 00:42:29,161
And the log probability of a whole answer is just the sum of the log probabilities of its

625
00:42:29,161 --> 00:42:34,573
tokens, which is exactly the negative of the loss that our model already computes.

626
00:42:34,573 --> 00:42:37,922
So in code, it's almost embarrassingly simple.

627
00:42:37,922 --> 00:42:39,815
Sample a bunch of answers.

628
00:42:39,815 --> 00:42:46,003
For each one, compute the sum of the log probabilities of its tokens, multiply by its

629
00:42:46,003 --> 00:42:48,842
reward, and do gradient ascent on that.

630
00:42:48,842 --> 00:42:55,394
And if you squint at it, it's basically SFT on your own samples, weighted by how good they

631
00:42:55,394 --> 00:42:55,758
were.

632
00:42:55,758 --> 00:42:57,337
But there's one problem.

633
00:42:57,337 --> 00:43:03,061
If the rewards are zero or one, then the wrong answers get a weight of zero, so they're

634
00:43:03,061 --> 00:43:08,916
just ignored, and every right answer gets pushed up, even on easy questions where all the

635
00:43:08,916 --> 00:43:10,166
answers were right.

636
00:43:10,166 --> 00:43:12,798
So what we do is we subtract a baseline.

637
00:43:12,798 --> 00:43:18,587
For every question, we sample a group of answers, say sixteen of them, and the advantage

638
00:43:18,587 --> 00:43:22,797
of each answer is its reward minus the mean reward of the group.

639
00:43:22,797 --> 00:43:28,653
So if an answer is better than average for this question, its probability gets pushed up.

640
00:43:28,653 --> 00:43:32,666
If it's worse than average, its probability gets pushed down.

641
00:43:32,666 --> 00:43:38,060
And you can show that subtracting a baseline like this doesn't change the expected

642
00:43:38,060 --> 00:43:41,416
gradient at all, it just makes it a lot less noisy.

643
00:43:41,416 --> 00:43:45,626
This is the "group" in GRPO, group relative policy optimization.

644
00:43:45,626 --> 00:43:51,704
Before we do this on a transformer, let's build some intuition on the tiniest possible

645
00:43:51,704 --> 00:43:52,269
example.

646
00:43:52,269 --> 00:43:58,417
Imagine a "language model" that only ever produces a single token, out of four options,

647
00:43:58,417 --> 00:44:00,608
and only option two is correct.

648
00:44:00,608 --> 00:44:02,728
The model is just four logits.

649
00:44:02,728 --> 00:44:08,876
We sample a group of sixteen attempts, reward the correct ones, compute the advantages,

650
00:44:08,876 --> 00:44:11,278
and do the policy gradient update.

651
00:44:12,278 --> 00:44:18,170
So the probability of the correct answer starts at a quarter, because the logits are all

652
00:44:18,170 --> 00:44:24,063
zero, and after only ten steps it's already at 85 percent, and it keeps climbing towards

653
00:44:24,063 --> 00:44:24,330
one.

654
00:44:24,330 --> 00:44:26,406
Nobody ever told it the answer.

655
00:44:26,406 --> 00:44:29,754
It just tried things, and did more of what worked.

656
00:44:29,754 --> 00:44:35,646
Now watch what happens if the model starts out really bad at this, if the correct answer

657
00:44:35,646 --> 00:44:37,722
is very unlikely to begin with.

658
00:44:38,722 --> 00:44:39,790
Nothing happens.

659
00:44:39,790 --> 00:44:45,668
The correct answer is never sampled, so the reward is always zero, so all the advantages

660
00:44:45,668 --> 00:44:48,406
are zero, and there's no gradient at all.

661
00:44:48,406 --> 00:44:54,150
And this is a really important lesson about RL: it can only amplify behaviors that the

662
00:44:54,150 --> 00:44:56,688
model already does at least sometimes.

663
00:44:56,688 --> 00:44:59,560
It can't conjure up something from nothing.

664
00:44:59,560 --> 00:45:05,371
That's why we do SFT first, to get the model into a regime where it sometimes succeeds,

665
00:45:05,371 --> 00:45:07,509
and then RL takes it from there.

666
00:45:07,509 --> 00:45:11,637
Exercise: Pause here and try to write the loss yourself.

667
00:45:11,637 --> 00:45:17,978
You have the inputs and targets of a batch of sixteen sampled answers, the targets are

668
00:45:17,978 --> 00:45:24,098
minus one wherever we shouldn't train, and you have a tensor of sixteen advantages.

669
00:45:24,098 --> 00:45:29,923
Our model can return the per-token loss if you pass loss_reduction equals none.

670
00:45:29,923 --> 00:45:32,429
Pause the video and give it a try.

671
00:45:34,429 --> 00:45:36,718
Okay, let's look at the code.

672
00:45:36,718 --> 00:45:38,296
First, the rollouts.

673
00:45:39,296 --> 00:45:45,388
So for every question, we render the prompt and use the engine to sample a whole group of

674
00:45:45,388 --> 00:45:49,975
answers in parallel, at temperature one, because we want diversity.

675
00:45:49,975 --> 00:45:56,068
We decode each one and get its reward from the task, and for SpellingBee and GSM8K that's

676
00:45:56,068 --> 00:45:59,902
just whether the answer after the hash signs is correct.

677
00:45:59,902 --> 00:46:05,926
Then we build inputs and targets exactly like in SFT, and here's where the engine's mask

678
00:46:05,926 --> 00:46:11,813
comes in: we set the targets to minus one for the prompt, for the padding, and for the

679
00:46:11,813 --> 00:46:15,099
tool outputs that were forced into the sequence.

680
00:46:15,099 --> 00:46:19,070
We only train on the tokens that the model actually chose.

681
00:46:19,070 --> 00:46:22,972
And then the advantages are the rewards minus their mean.

682
00:46:22,972 --> 00:46:25,323
And here's the training step.

683
00:46:26,323 --> 00:46:32,003
So the model gives us the per-token loss, which is the negative log probability, so we

684
00:46:32,003 --> 00:46:37,816
negate it to get the log probabilities, and the ignored tokens have a log probability of

685
00:46:37,816 --> 00:46:38,674
exactly zero.

686
00:46:38,674 --> 00:46:44,619
We multiply each token's log probability by the advantage of the answer it belongs to, sum

687
00:46:44,619 --> 00:46:48,251
it all up, and normalize by the number of valid tokens.

688
00:46:48,251 --> 00:46:54,196
And then the loss is the negative of that objective, because PyTorch minimizes and we want

689
00:46:54,196 --> 00:46:54,988
to maximize.

690
00:46:54,988 --> 00:46:55,913
And that's it.

691
00:46:55,913 --> 00:46:57,696
That's the whole algorithm.

692
00:46:57,696 --> 00:47:04,060
Now, if you've read about GRPO or PPO, you might be wondering where all the other stuff

693
00:47:04,060 --> 00:47:04,279
is.

694
00:47:04,279 --> 00:47:10,716
And nanochat makes four deliberate simplifications, and they're written right at the top

695
00:47:10,716 --> 00:47:11,593
of the file.

696
00:47:12,593 --> 00:47:16,412
Number one, there's no KL penalty to a reference model.

697
00:47:16,412 --> 00:47:22,661
That's a regularizer that keeps the model from drifting too far from the SFT model, and in

698
00:47:22,661 --> 00:47:26,827
practice, for a short run like this, it's not really needed.

699
00:47:26,827 --> 00:47:30,020
Number two, there's no PPO ratio and clipping.

700
00:47:30,020 --> 00:47:36,200
Those exist because in PPO you take multiple gradient steps on the same batch of samples,

701
00:47:36,200 --> 00:47:41,337
so the samples become stale, off-policy, and you need to correct for that.

702
00:47:41,337 --> 00:47:47,586
But we take exactly one gradient step per batch, so we are always on-policy, and the ratio

703
00:47:47,586 --> 00:47:49,530
would always be exactly one.

704
00:47:49,530 --> 00:47:55,571
Number three, we normalize by the total number of tokens, not per answer, which is what

705
00:47:55,571 --> 00:48:00,361
the DAPO paper recommends, so that long answers aren't down-weighted.

706
00:48:00,361 --> 00:48:06,471
And number four, the advantage is just the reward minus the mean, we don't divide by the

707
00:48:06,471 --> 00:48:07,790
standard deviation.

708
00:48:07,790 --> 00:48:13,831
Put all of that together and what you're left with is honestly just good old REINFORCE,

709
00:48:13,831 --> 00:48:15,705
with a group mean baseline.

710
00:48:15,705 --> 00:48:17,409
One more thing to notice.

711
00:48:17,409 --> 00:48:23,404
If all sixteen answers to a question are correct, or all of them are wrong, then all the

712
00:48:23,404 --> 00:48:28,173
advantages are zero, and that question contributes no gradient at all.

713
00:48:28,173 --> 00:48:34,032
So RL only learns from questions that are at the edge of the model's ability, where it

714
00:48:34,032 --> 00:48:36,689
sometimes succeeds and sometimes fails.

715
00:48:36,689 --> 00:48:42,412
If your model can never solve a problem, RL can't help you, and that's why we do SFT

716
00:48:42,412 --> 00:48:42,820
first.

717
00:48:42,820 --> 00:48:44,442
Okay, so let's run it.

718
00:48:44,442 --> 00:48:51,003
We start from our SFT model, which gets about half of the SpellingBee questions right, so

719
00:48:51,003 --> 00:48:56,458
it's right at the edge of its ability, which is exactly where RL can help.

720
00:48:56,458 --> 00:49:02,577
On a GPU you'd use the defaults, sixteen questions per step and sixteen samples per

721
00:49:02,577 --> 00:49:03,240
question.

722
00:49:03,240 --> 00:49:09,874
On my CPU, I'm going to do 40 steps, with eight questions per step and eight samples each,

723
00:49:09,874 --> 00:49:16,288
so 64 attempts per step, and every ten steps we evaluate on 48 held-out questions, with

724
00:49:16,288 --> 00:49:17,688
eight samples each.

725
00:49:17,688 --> 00:49:21,743
This took about three hours, most of it spent sampling.

726
00:49:24,243 --> 00:49:25,672
So look at the eval.

727
00:49:25,672 --> 00:49:28,316
Before RL, pass at one is 44 percent.

728
00:49:28,316 --> 00:49:34,034
That's the fraction of individual samples, at temperature one, that are correct.

729
00:49:34,034 --> 00:49:40,394
But pass at eight is 92 percent: if we let the model try eight times, then for 92 percent

730
00:49:40,394 --> 00:49:44,325
of the questions at least one of the attempts is right.

731
00:49:44,325 --> 00:49:50,328
So the model already "knows" how to solve almost all of these, it's just unreliable.

732
00:49:50,328 --> 00:49:56,617
And then after only ten steps of RL, pass at one shoots up to 84 percent, and by the end

733
00:49:56,617 --> 00:49:58,189
it's at 87 and a half.

734
00:49:58,189 --> 00:50:00,905
And pass at eight goes to 100 percent.

735
00:50:00,905 --> 00:50:05,122
This is a really common pattern with RL on language models.

736
00:50:05,122 --> 00:50:11,554
A lot of what it does is take the things the model can do some of the time, and make it do

737
00:50:11,554 --> 00:50:13,126
them most of the time.

738
00:50:13,126 --> 00:50:15,913
It converts pass at k into pass at one.

739
00:50:15,913 --> 00:50:22,215
The training reward on the other hand is really noisy, look at step 26, where it dips to

740
00:50:22,215 --> 00:50:22,573
0.34.

741
00:50:22,573 --> 00:50:28,588
That's because every step only has eight questions, so some steps just happen to get

742
00:50:28,588 --> 00:50:29,519
harder words.

743
00:50:29,519 --> 00:50:33,959
Always look at a held out evaluation, not the training reward.

744
00:50:33,959 --> 00:50:40,332
And one more fun thing: the number of sampled tokens per answer goes from around 210 down

745
00:50:40,332 --> 00:50:41,263
to about 155.

746
00:50:41,263 --> 00:50:43,912
Nobody asked the model to be shorter.

747
00:50:43,912 --> 00:50:50,286
But a lot of the long answers were ones where it got lost, misspelling a word into a long

748
00:50:50,286 --> 00:50:56,301
garbage sequence of letters and never getting back on track, and those tend to get a

749
00:50:56,301 --> 00:50:59,093
reward of zero, so RL pushes them down.

750
00:50:59,093 --> 00:51:06,774
And if we use our eval script, with greedy decoding on 100 test questions, SpellingBee

751
00:51:06,774 --> 00:51:12,311
goes from 53 percent for the SFT model to 86 percent after RL.

752
00:51:12,311 --> 00:51:16,253
And now, finally, let's ask it the famous question.

753
00:51:16,253 --> 00:51:22,978
I'll take eight samples from both the SFT model and the RL model, and print their final

754
00:51:22,978 --> 00:51:23,596
answers.

755
00:51:24,596 --> 00:51:26,791
Okay, so look at the first one.

756
00:51:26,791 --> 00:51:33,094
The SFT model gets three r's in strawberry only once out of eight tries, and the RL model

757
00:51:33,094 --> 00:51:35,855
gets it right eight out of eight times.

758
00:51:35,855 --> 00:51:41,025
Same thing for cheese, two out of eight before, eight out of eight after.

759
00:51:41,025 --> 00:51:41,733
That's RL.

760
00:51:41,733 --> 00:51:43,722
But now look at the last one.

761
00:51:43,722 --> 00:51:48,798
"How many r are in strawberry", without the quotes and without "the word".

762
00:51:48,798 --> 00:51:53,120
And both models fall apart, the answers are all over the place.

763
00:51:53,120 --> 00:51:54,218
What's going on?

764
00:51:54,218 --> 00:51:57,511
Let's look at what the RL model actually writes.

765
00:51:58,511 --> 00:52:02,661
It says, we are asked to find the number r in the word "sweet".

766
00:52:02,661 --> 00:52:03,056
Sweet!

767
00:52:03,056 --> 00:52:05,362
It didn't even copy the right word.

768
00:52:05,362 --> 00:52:08,194
And the tokenization tells you exactly why.

769
00:52:08,194 --> 00:52:13,596
In the first version, the word is "space strawberry", a single token, one integer.

770
00:52:13,596 --> 00:52:19,327
In the version with quotes, it's "st", "raw", "berry", exactly the same tokens that the

771
00:52:19,327 --> 00:52:23,280
model has to write inside its quotes and in the python call.

772
00:52:23,280 --> 00:52:28,616
So in the quoted version, the model just has to copy three tokens, which is easy.

773
00:52:28,616 --> 00:52:34,347
But in the unquoted version, it has to know that the single token "space strawberry" is

774
00:52:34,347 --> 00:52:39,814
spelled s, t, r, a, w, and so on, and write it out as a completely different set of

775
00:52:39,814 --> 00:52:40,275
tokens.

776
00:52:40,275 --> 00:52:41,922
And it doesn't know that.

777
00:52:41,922 --> 00:52:46,007
So it reaches for something that's nearby in meaning, "sweet".

778
00:52:46,007 --> 00:52:51,804
Our SpellingBee training data mostly has rare words, which get split into several tokens

779
00:52:51,804 --> 00:52:57,535
either way, so the model never really had to learn how to unpack a single common token.

780
00:52:57,535 --> 00:53:00,697
And this is the tokenizer biting us, once again.

781
00:53:00,697 --> 00:53:06,625
If you remember the tokenization video, so many of the weird failures of LLMs come back to

782
00:53:06,625 --> 00:53:09,590
the tokenizer, and this is a perfect example.

783
00:53:09,590 --> 00:53:11,243
So how would we fix this?

784
00:53:11,243 --> 00:53:16,931
Well, now that we understand the failure, we could add training data for exactly this:

785
00:53:16,931 --> 00:53:19,378
common words that are a single token.

786
00:53:19,378 --> 00:53:22,289
And that's how this stuff works in practice.

787
00:53:22,289 --> 00:53:27,911
You look at the failures, you figure out why they happen, and you go back and fix the

788
00:53:27,911 --> 00:53:28,241
data.

789
00:53:28,241 --> 00:53:31,274
Exercise: Look at the samples during RL.

790
00:53:31,274 --> 00:53:37,644
Does the model start trusting python over its manual count, or the other way around?

791
00:53:37,644 --> 00:53:44,242
Try changing the reward, for example a small penalty for long answers, and see what the

792
00:53:44,242 --> 00:53:45,076
model does.

793
00:53:45,076 --> 00:53:49,095
Be careful, RL will find any loophole in your reward.

794
00:53:49,095 --> 00:53:51,673
Pause the video and give it a try.

795
00:53:55,173 --> 00:53:56,923
Okay, so that's everything.

796
00:53:56,923 --> 00:53:58,542
Let's zoom out and recap.

797
00:53:58,542 --> 00:54:02,949
We took a base model, a document simulator, and we did three things.

798
00:54:02,949 --> 00:54:07,808
We defined a format for conversations, with special tokens and a loss mask.

799
00:54:07,808 --> 00:54:13,446
We did supervised finetuning on a mixture of conversations, where every dataset teaches

800
00:54:13,446 --> 00:54:18,888
something specific: chatting, multiple choice, math with a calculator, and spelling.

801
00:54:18,888 --> 00:54:24,461
And then we did reinforcement learning, where the model samples its own answers and we

802
00:54:24,461 --> 00:54:26,210
push up the ones that work.

803
00:54:26,210 --> 00:54:31,912
Along the way we wrote an inference engine with a KV cache and tool use, and evaluations

804
00:54:31,912 --> 00:54:33,338
to measure everything.

805
00:54:33,338 --> 00:54:37,916
Now everything we wrote today maps one to one onto nanochat.

806
00:54:37,916 --> 00:54:44,782
Our tokenizer is nanochat's tokenizer.py, our tasks are its tasks directory, our engine is

807
00:54:44,782 --> 00:54:50,962
its engine.py, and our chat_sft, chat_rl, chat_eval and chat_cli are its scripts.

808
00:54:50,962 --> 00:54:54,701
The real thing has a few more bells and whistles.

809
00:54:54,701 --> 00:55:01,567
The base model is pretrained from scratch instead of GPT-2, and you can pick its size with

810
00:55:01,567 --> 00:55:03,475
a single depth parameter.

811
00:55:03,475 --> 00:55:10,112
It uses the Muon optimizer for the matrices, it runs in bfloat16 across eight GPUs with

812
00:55:10,112 --> 00:55:16,445
distributed data parallel, and it warm-starts the optimizer state from pretraining.

813
00:55:16,445 --> 00:55:19,268
And in nanochat, RL is done on GSM8K.

814
00:55:19,268 --> 00:55:23,083
But the ideas are exactly the ones we built today.

815
00:55:23,083 --> 00:55:27,202
If you have a GPU node, the whole thing is one script.

816
00:55:28,202 --> 00:55:30,888
And there's a lot that we didn't cover.

817
00:55:30,888 --> 00:55:36,122
We only did reinforcement learning on problems where we can check the answer

818
00:55:36,122 --> 00:55:37,086
automatically.

819
00:55:37,086 --> 00:55:39,910
But what about writing a poem, or a joke?

820
00:55:39,910 --> 00:55:42,045
There's no answer key for that.

821
00:55:42,045 --> 00:55:47,899
And that's where RLHF comes in, reinforcement learning from human feedback, where you

822
00:55:47,899 --> 00:55:53,821
train a separate reward model on human preferences, and then do RL against that reward

823
00:55:53,821 --> 00:55:54,235
model.

824
00:55:54,235 --> 00:55:58,229
Or methods like DPO, which skip the reward model entirely.

825
00:55:58,229 --> 00:56:04,014
But be careful, because a reward model is itself a neural network, and if you run RL

826
00:56:04,014 --> 00:56:09,523
against it for too long, the policy will find adversarial examples that fool it.

827
00:56:09,523 --> 00:56:15,584
So RLHF is a bit of a different beast from the kind of RL we did today, where the reward

828
00:56:15,584 --> 00:56:17,443
is the actual ground truth.

829
00:56:17,443 --> 00:56:23,433
And there's the whole world of reasoning models, where you run RL for a very long time on

830
00:56:23,433 --> 00:56:29,490
hard math and code problems, and the model discovers on its own that it should think for a

831
00:56:29,490 --> 00:56:34,471
long time, try different approaches, backtrack, and double check its work.

832
00:56:34,471 --> 00:56:38,576
That's really what our SpellingBee task is a tiny cartoon of.

833
00:56:38,576 --> 00:56:40,572
Okay, so that's it for today.

834
00:56:40,572 --> 00:56:42,085
I hope you enjoyed it.

835
00:56:42,085 --> 00:56:47,865
Take the code, run it, break it, add your own tasks, and see what your model learns.

836
00:56:47,865 --> 00:56:49,104
See you next time.
